WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:06.859
Hi, I'm Lisa Manning. I'm an assistant
professor at Syracuse University.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:18.230
So we've been interested in trying to
understand how groups of cells interact

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.910
with one another mechanically to
generate properties of biological

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.800
tissues and processes like development.
This is really important because it

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.269
turns out that lots of cells have
signaling molecules that can measure

00:00:32.269 --> 00:00:36.379
things about their mechanical
environment and respond to it and that's

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:40.879
one way of programming interesting
patterns during development. And in

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:45.049
particular we've been interested in
understanding whether groups of cells

00:00:45.049 --> 00:00:50.269
behave more like a fluid or more like a
solid and it turns out that the tools of

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:56.930
Glassy physics of glassy material
science help us to understand how groups

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:02.780
of disordered things whether they be
atoms or they be cells can transition

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.820
from a fluid like behavior to a solid
like behavior and so we've been thinking

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:13.939
about this problem and we've learned
that when groups of cells change single

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:18.440
cell properties things like the number
of caderents which is the adhesive

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:24.410
molecules that help cells stick together
or the expression levels of actin or

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:28.850
myosin which help give the cells
mechanical integrity they can actually

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:35.000
drive the entire tissue from a fluid to
a solid and those transitions can help

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.460
shape organs into functional structures
and they can help change the way the

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.020
tissue responds to mechanical forces and
so we think that this shows a lot of

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.750
promise for understanding how organs
change their shape during development,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:55.540
with implications for congenital disease.

